Feature: ERP Communication Hub — Slack-Inspired Collaboration Module1. Objective
Implement a production-ready Communication Hub inside the existing ERP application.
The module should provide Slack-inspired employee messaging, channels, persistent notifications, and an integrated approval inbox.
This is an enhancement to an existing enterprise ERP, not a new application.
2. Mandatory rules before making changes
First inspect the existing repository, architecture, database schema, Supabase configuration, authentication flow, employee model, authorization helpers, approval workflows, navigation, shared UI components, and design system.
Before coding:
Identify the existing employee/user identity source and how authenticated user IDs map to employee records.
Identify the existing approval engine and its supported server-side actions.
Identify existing notifications, activity logs, audit tables, event handlers, and reusable messaging components, if any.
Identify the current Next.js routing structure, server/client component conventions, Supabase client utilities, Zod patterns, and database migration workflow.
Identify existing shared DataTable, status badge, dialog, dropdown, form, toast, and responsive layout components.
Check for overlapping functionality and reuse it instead of creating duplicates.
Do not assume table names, user ID formats, route names, or database relationships before inspecting the code.
Create a concise implementation plan based on the actual repository. Then implement the feature in manageable phases.
Do not replace existing authentication, create duplicate employee records, bypass existing ERP permissions, or introduce a parallel approval engine.
3. Technical constraints
Use the technologies already established in the repository, including where applicable:
Next.js and React
TypeScript
Supabase PostgreSQL
Supabase Auth and Realtime
Supabase Storage
Existing ERP authentication and authorization
Existing Zod validation patterns
Existing Tailwind CSS and shared UI components
Existing lucide-react icon library
Follow the repository's installed package versions and established conventions. Do not upgrade dependencies or change framework architecture without a demonstrated need.
4. Module navigation
Add a Communication Hub entry to the existing ERP navigation and permission-aware routing system.
Provide these views:
Messages
Channels
Notifications
My Approvals
Saved Messages
Search
Use the existing application shell, responsive layout, breadcrumbs, page headers, typography, spacing, theme tokens, dialogs, and notification components.
Use the existing ERP color system, including the established peacock color #087F8C, semantic status colors, and neutral surfaces. Do not introduce an unrelated visual design system.
5. Phase 1 — Core messagingDirect messages
Search and select existing ERP employees.
Create or retrieve the correct one-to-one conversation.
Prevent duplicate direct conversations where the current design requires uniqueness.
Send and receive text messages.
Display sender, timestamp, message content, and delivery/loading/error states.
Load message history with cursor-based pagination.
Support optimistic UI updates with rollback or retry on failure.
Persist unread/read state per user.
Group conversations
Create group conversations.
Add and remove participants according to appropriate permissions.
Display participant details from existing employee records.
Enforce membership checks on the server and database.
Handle removed participants without exposing future messages to them.
Channels
Support public and private channels with explicitly defined access rules.
Support department and project channels.
Reuse existing department and project relationships when available.
Allow authorized users to create channels and manage membership.
Implement channel membership, message history, unread counts, and live updates.
Ensure private channel messages cannot be read by non-members.
Message interaction
Reply to a message using a thread.
Mention employees using an explicit supported mention format.
Display reactions and unread indicators.
Add edit and delete functionality with clearly defined ownership and authorization rules.
Support saved messages and pinned messages where appropriate.
Provide empty, loading, failure, and retry states.
6. Phase 2 — Notifications
Implement persistent, per-user notifications for relevant events:
Direct messages
Group and channel mentions
Assigned approval tasks
Approval decisions
Relevant ERP workflow updates
Other explicitly configured events
Requirements:
Store notifications in PostgreSQL.
Track read/unread state and creation time.
Support marking one notification or all notifications as read.
Provide navigation to the linked conversation or ERP record.
Deduplicate notifications using stable event identifiers.
Use Supabase Realtime for timely updates without treating Realtime as the permanent data store.
Provide reconnect and refresh recovery.
Avoid sending notifications to unauthorized recipients.
Respect user notification preferences where supported.
Integrate with existing notification infrastructure rather than creating duplicate notifications if a suitable system already exists.
7. Phase 2 — ERP approval inbox
Implement a My Approvals interface that displays actionable approval tasks from the existing ERP workflow system.
Display, as available:
Approval item and document reference
Module and document type
Requester
Submission time
Current workflow status
Pending approver or approval stage
A link to open the source ERP record
Supported actions such as Approve or Reject
Critical rules:
The existing ERP workflow remains the authoritative source of approval state.
Do not create an independent approval engine.
Do not treat a notification or chat message as an authorization to approve.
Invoke existing approval services or server-side actions.
Revalidate the authenticated actor, record permissions, current workflow state, and required business rules on every action.
Require a reason when the existing workflow requires one.
Prevent duplicate or stale decisions and handle concurrent approvals safely.
Record decisions through the existing audit and workflow mechanisms.
Refresh the inbox from the authoritative workflow state.
Never report success before the server confirms the operation.
If the existing approval system cannot support an operation, document the missing integration point rather than implementing a misleading substitute.
8. Phase 3 — ERP record linking
Allow users to link authorized ERP records in messages and conversations.
Potential record types include:
Purchase requisitions and purchase orders
Vendor quotations and quotation comparisons
Inventory transactions
HR records
Finance records
Other existing ERP modules
Reuse existing route and record identifiers. Validate the entity type, target record, and user's access before resolving or opening a link.
Display a compact record preview containing only fields the current user is authorized to see.
A link to a record must not grant access to that record. Recheck permissions when opening it.
Avoid copying sensitive ERP document contents into unrestricted chat messages or notification payloads.
9. Database and migrations
Inspect the existing database before deciding what to create.
Reuse existing tables where suitable. Introduce only necessary new tables and migrations.
Evaluate the need for:
Channels
