Persistent notifications
Saved messages
ERP entity links
Approval inbox projection, only if needed
Reliable event delivery and deduplication
Audit integration, using existing audit infrastructure where possible
Use foreign keys, appropriate uniqueness constraints, indexes, timestamps, and explicit status constraints.
Use the established migration and deployment workflow. Do not modify production data or apply destructive schema changes without authorization.
10. Security requirements
Implement authorization at the server and database levels.
Reuse the existing ERP authentication system.
Derive the current actor from the trusted authenticated session.
Never trust a browser-provided sender ID, role, department, or approval permission.
Apply Row Level Security to applicable tables.
Verify conversation/channel membership for every relevant read and write.
Protect attachments using private storage and controlled access.
Validate uploaded file type and size.
Do not expose service-role credentials or privileged secrets to the browser.
Restrict administrative operations to authorized roles.
Prevent unauthorized access through guessed IDs, manipulated requests, direct URLs, and Realtime subscriptions.
Keep audit information accurate and protected.
Ensure notification recipients are authorized to know about the underlying event.
Follow the existing tenant/institution isolation model if the ERP supports multiple institutions.
Review RLS policies for both intended access and unauthorized access. Do not rely solely on hiding UI elements.
